.TH TIMES 2 "February 2026" "Substrate" "System Calls Manual"
.SH NAME
times \- get process times
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <sys/times.h>
.PP
.BI "clock_t times(struct tms *" buf ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR times ()
stores the current process times in the
.I struct tms
pointed to by
.IR buf .
.PP
.I struct tms
is defined in
.I <sys/times.h>
as follows:
.PP
.RS
.nf
struct tms {
    clock_t tms_utime;  /* user time */
    clock_t tms_stime;  /* system time */
    clock_t tms_cutime; /* user time of children */
    clock_t tms_cstime; /* system time of children */
};
.fi
.RE
.PP
The
.I tms_utime
field contains the CPU time spent executing instructions of the calling
process.  The
.I tms_stime
field contains the CPU time spent in the system while executing on behalf
of the calling process.  The
.I tms_cutime
field contains the sum of the
.I tms_utime
and
.I tms_cutime
values for all waited-for terminated children.  The
.I tms_cstime
field contains the sum of the
.I tms_stime
and
.I tms_cstime
values for all waited-for terminated children.
.PP
Times for terminated children (and their descendants) are added in at the
moment
.BR wait (2)
or
.BR waitpid (2)
returns their process ID.  In particular, times of grandchildren that the
children did not wait for are never seen.
.PP
All times returned are in clock ticks.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR times ()
returns the number of clock ticks that have elapsed since an arbitrary
point in the past.  The return value may overflow the possible range of
type
.IR clock_t .
On error, \-1 is returned, and
.I errno
is set appropriately.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EFAULT
.I buf
points outside the process's accessible address space.
.SH CONFORMING TO
POSIX.1-2001, POSIX.1-2008, SVr4, 4.3BSD.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR time (1),
.BR getrusage (2),
.BR wait (2),
.BR clock (3),
.BR sysconf (3)
.SH COLOPHON
This page is part of the Substrate operating system documentation.
